{"from": "site:pool-of-bethesda", "type": "attests", "to": "place:bethesda-pool", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated twin pools in the St Anne's compound, first identified by Conrad Schick in the 19th century and further excavated in 1964, are the accepted site of the pool of Bethesda ('seems plausibly to fit' John's description, per the fetched Wikipedia article); the seed lead grades the topographic match B."}
{"from": "site:pool-of-bethesda", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-02-bethesda-pool", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple-period reservoir and twin pools excavated at St Anne's match 'a pool by the Sheep Gate' with porticoes; the five-porticoes arrangement itself was not verifiable on a fetched page."}
{"from": "site:pool-of-bethesda", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-03-sick-multitude", "grade": "C", "note": "Murphy-O'Connor's reconstruction: the caves east of the two pools became small baths with a religious/medical function from the 1st century BC - a healing sanctuary plausibly explaining sick crowds at the water; an inference from remains, not proof of the multitude."}
{"from": "site:pool-of-bethesda", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:jn-05-03-sick-multitude", "note": "The same 1st-century healing baths were an Asclepeion (one pool sacred to Fortuna), a pagan healing cult that offers an alternative background for the 'moving of the water' tradition - a verse (5:4) that P66, P75, Sinaiticus and Vaticanus omit."}
{"from": "site:pool-of-bethesda", "type": "identified_as", "to": "place:bethesda-pool", "note": "The identification is generally accepted but stated cautiously ('seems plausibly to fit'); earlier alternatives (Fountain of the Virgin, Birket Israel, the Struthion Pool) were rejected on the fetched article."}
{"from": "artifact:portico-capital-bethesda", "type": "attests", "to": "place:bethesda-pool", "grade": "D", "note": "A fetched photograph caption records a capital segment 'from the portico that surrounded the pool' now in Memphis, USA; its date and find-context are unstated on the page, so the grade is D and it cannot secure the five porticoes of John 5:2."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shabbat-7-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-10-carrying-mat-sabbath", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Shabbat 7:2's thirty-ninth primary category of Sabbath labour is 'one who carries out an object from domain to domain' - the same carrying practice in the same city; the Mishnah was redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly. (The seed lead suggested B with the caveat; the calibration rule for later-period finds and the Matthew 12 precedent give C.) Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-33-john-testified", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus independently attests John the Baptist as a preacher commanding crowds to baptism - a public prophetic witness in the same period and region; he never says John testified about Jesus, so the witness role, not the testimony's content, is attested."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-01-feast-jerusalem", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple stood as Jerusalem's temple through the 30s CE, the destination of festival pilgrimage; it attests the setting, not that this journey happened at this feast."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-6-422-425", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-01-feast-jerusalem", "grade": "C", "note": "Cestius Gallus' census counted a vast Passover pilgrimage to Jerusalem (c. 66 CE): festival pilgrimage on a huge scale, a generation after the 20s CE and for the Passover, not the unnamed feast of 5:1."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-05-14-man-in-temple", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's temple stood in Jerusalem throughout the 30s CE; it attests the setting of the encounter, not the encounter itself."}
